Reading: for N >> r the only region is [0, p*) and the bisection is well-posed.  For r comparable to N
(very long runs of correct predictions, where P'_global is already ~1) a second region appears near the
pole r+1-rx=0, and a [0,1] bisection may land in it; the text gives no bounds, so this is a property of
the equation as written, not of this implementation.

CORRECTION (written after reading the measurement above, 2026-11-07): the last paragraph was drafted
before the numbers and is NOT supported by them.  Measured: all 17 (r, N) pairs, including r = N+1
(38, 37), (90, 100) and (500, 1000), show a SINGLE initial above-target interval ending at the
bisection's P_local; no second region appeared at a grid spacing of 2.5e-6.  The r=1, N=999937 row
reports "none" only because its root (1.005e-8) lies below the first grid point.  The pole at
r + 1 - r x = 0 therefore remains a SUSPICION about widths below grid resolution, not a measured
effect, and the bisection on [0, 1] is well-posed on everything measured here.
